WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:02.480
We're going to talk about testing and Python.

00:02.480 --> 00:07.320
Tests are a cornerstone of a solid Python program.

00:07.320 --> 00:10.320
And thankfully, because of Python's batteries

00:10.320 --> 00:14.040
included philosophy, everything that we need to write unit tests

00:14.040 --> 00:16.560
is included in the standard library.

00:16.560 --> 00:21.320
So let's learn about the basics of unit testing.

00:21.320 --> 00:24.960
Unit testing is a method of testing software methods

00:24.960 --> 00:29.520
where individual functions are tested in an automatic fashion

00:29.520 --> 00:33.480
to determine if they work as expected.

00:33.480 --> 00:37.600
These automated tests, they help you find bugs quicker

00:37.600 --> 00:42.960
and faster than not testing at all or even manual testing.

00:42.960 --> 00:46.200
And you can also write them alongside or before you

00:46.200 --> 00:50.320
write your functions in a style called test-driven development

00:50.320 --> 00:54.400
to help your code be as bug free as possible.

00:54.400 --> 00:58.680
There are three types of automated tests

00:58.680 --> 01:03.040
that can be performed at different levels of abstraction.

01:03.040 --> 01:06.960
Unit tests operate on the small testable unit of code,

01:06.960 --> 01:13.040
usually a function that does a single action or an operation.

01:13.040 --> 01:14.840
Integration tests will check to see

01:14.840 --> 01:18.720
of different units or modules of code work together

01:18.720 --> 01:19.960
as a group.

01:19.960 --> 01:23.680
And then functional tests operate on units of functionality

01:23.680 --> 01:27.400
to make sure that a specific function of the software is working.

01:27.400 --> 01:29.360
And it may involve a lot of unit tests

01:29.360 --> 01:33.000
or whole systems working together.

01:33.000 --> 01:36.800
For this class, I'm just going to show you how to write unit tests,

01:36.800 --> 01:39.520
but know that testing the real world

01:39.520 --> 01:43.720
are pretty essential for your Python production programs.

01:43.720 --> 01:47.680
They're very helpful because many companies

01:47.680 --> 01:51.360
use a style of development called CICD

01:51.360 --> 01:55.200
or continuous integration continuous deployment.

01:55.200 --> 01:58.280
That means a lot of unit tests, integration tests,

01:58.280 --> 02:03.280
maybe even a few functional tests, which run automatically

02:03.280 --> 02:10.680
before your code is merged in with the deployed master branch.

02:10.680 --> 02:13.880
If the test fail, the deployment can be stopped

02:13.880 --> 02:15.680
or not happen at all.

02:15.680 --> 02:17.800
The developers can be notified.

02:17.800 --> 02:19.720
And your broken code doesn't make it

02:19.720 --> 02:22.360
into your production servers.

02:22.360 --> 02:27.760
But this technique only works if you write a lot of good tests.

02:27.760 --> 02:30.400
It can be a bit complicated to set this up properly,

02:30.400 --> 02:33.440
depending on which deployment strategy you're using,

02:33.440 --> 02:37.880
but it really saves you an enormous amount of time in the long run

02:37.880 --> 02:41.640
and helps your users to not see bugs.

02:41.640 --> 02:49.640
So before we talk about unit tests and solves,

02:49.640 --> 02:54.720
Python comes with something called the assert keyword.

02:54.720 --> 02:59.760
And that kind of helps you do a little bit of quick sanity checking.

02:59.760 --> 03:03.640
In assertion, just checks condition, the Boolean value

03:03.640 --> 03:09.120
of a condition to see if it's true or false, if it matches.

03:09.120 --> 03:12.880
If the assertion is true, the program will continue.

03:12.880 --> 03:14.680
If it's false, you'll see an assertion error

03:14.680 --> 03:16.560
and your program will stop.

03:16.560 --> 03:19.360
So let's say, going back to my repal.

03:22.360 --> 03:28.040
Let's say, I have a number A. I can type assert A

03:28.040 --> 03:32.040
is greater than 0 when that pass is just fine.

03:32.040 --> 03:37.920
If I assert that A is less than 0, I'm going to get an assertion error.

03:37.920 --> 03:41.640
Know that assertion errors are for sanity checking,

03:41.640 --> 03:43.560
they're not for production.

03:43.560 --> 03:45.960
Simple checks, sanity checks.

03:45.960 --> 03:49.280
They're not for validation.

03:49.280 --> 03:52.720
Certainly not for validating any type of user input

03:52.720 --> 03:58.320
or determining if a file isn't found something like that.

03:58.320 --> 04:04.160
These situations are better suited for raising an actual exception.

04:04.160 --> 04:06.360
And the other thing about assertions

04:06.360 --> 04:10.760
is they're not guaranteed to be run in your production code.

04:10.760 --> 04:15.800
It's easy to turn off, disable all assertions at runtime,

04:15.800 --> 04:19.680
just by starting your Python program with a particular flag.

04:19.680 --> 04:23.680
So never rely on assertions to run on production code.

04:23.680 --> 04:25.920
Never use them for validation.

04:25.920 --> 04:29.960
Never use them for user input validation.

04:29.960 --> 04:31.720
Makes sense?

